ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: red
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [
  "Amplification rate = re-store rate is a TAUTOLOGY of the generative process, not a discovered phenomenon: writes are sampled with probability p of being a repeat from a finite urn, so 'fraction-redundant ≈ p' is the expected value by construction (and pins at 1-1/N once the urn saturates). The '0.197 @restore=0.2, 0.399 @0.4, 0.601 @0.6' table is literally recovering the input parameter. No emergent write-amplification is demonstrated — the experiment measures its own knob.",
  "The dedup-vs-size-cap recall delta is the expected outcome of a basic coupon-collector / cache-replacement argument: when the query distribution is heavy-tailed over OLD facts (long-horizon) and the redundancy rate is high, ANY content-aware policy will beat a pure-recency cap; when queries favor recent facts (short-horizon), recency wins. This is textbook LRU-vs-LFU territory (Belady 1966; ARC, Megiddo & Modha 2003) — no novel mechanism, just a re-derivation in an agent-memory wrapper.",
  "The 'tuned threshold' result is just the precision/recall operating point of the underlying similarity signal — Jaccard at thr=0.3 has precision 0.37 (collapses distinct items), at thr=0.7 it has recall 0.4 under paraphrase. The 'win' at thr=0.5 is selecting the F1-optimal point on the gate's ROC, then claiming the policy beats baseline at that point. ROC-point cherry-picking, not a robust effect."
]
CONFOUNDS: [
  "Saturation artifact in the main grid (universe=250, T=1000) drove the headline 0.75 amplification — researcher flagged honestly, but this means the original pre-reg evidence for (A) was confounded by urn size, not by any property of agent memory.",
  "Query distribution is set by the simulator (horizon ∈ {long, short}) and directly determines which baseline wins — dedup's advantage is fully explained by 'fraction of queries that hit old facts.' Without a justified prior on real agent query distributions, the win/loss split is a choice of confound, not a finding.",
  "Cap value (120 vs 200) interacts with universe size and re-store rate to determine whether size-cap is even binding. The 'matched-size' comparison is only meaningful if the cap binds for both policies in the same regime — not separately verified per-config.",
  "fact_id == 'duplicate' is itself an assumption: in real agent traces, two writes with the same latent fact may legitimately encode different temporal/contextual updates ('Alice lives in NYC' at t=0 vs t=100 after she moved). The simulator's ground-truth notion of redundancy may not match the downstream task's notion."
]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [
  "That the re-store rate in real MemGPT/Letta/Generative-Agents traces clears 25% — the L0 cannot answer this; the prereg acknowledged but the claim still asserts '>25%'.",
  "That a single threshold generalizes across corpora (the prereg explicitly screened against tune-a-knob; the result violates that screen and the disposition doesn't retract the claim's robustness).",
  "That Jaccard is a faithful proxy for embedding cosine — oracle gap mean +0.12 / max +0.80 says it is NOT; the L0 essentially measures Jaccard-as-gate, and any conclusion about 'pre-write semantic dedup' as a class is unjustified extrapolation.",
  "That recency size-cap is the strongest baseline. LRU, LFU, ARC, importance-weighted eviction (the actual MemGPT/Generative-Agents approach) are not tested — the claim 'beats size-cap' is the weakest possible bar and was never the relevant comparator."
]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [
  "thr=0.3 CI [-0.048, +0.072] includes 0 — reported but the headline 'wins 90/108 configs' obscures that the WIN COUNT is across pooled configs and conflates effect-sign with effect-size. No per-regime FDR correction across 540 policy rows × 3 thresholds.",
  "Only 5 seeds; per-config CIs are bootstrap over seeds, but the headline +0.103 / +0.113 numbers pool across heterogeneous configs (paraphrase × horizon × cap) where the true effect is known to flip sign. Pooled mean across regimes with sign-flipping effects is not a meaningful estimand.",
  "No power analysis, no pre-specified primary endpoint among (A)/(B); (A) is now reported as 'depends on universe size', which is post-hoc reframing after the saturation artifact was discovered.",
  "No reproducibility across an independent random generator / corpus; the fact-universe model is one stylized choice and the entire result rides on it.",
  "Variance of the oracle gap (mean 0.12, max 0.80) implies a heavy-tailed distribution — reporting only mean/max hides whether the gate is usable in tail regimes."
]
RATIONALE: The claim, as decomposed, fails on theoretical grounds before the empirics matter. Part (A) is a tautology: amplification ≈ re-store rate is the simulator's input parameter, not a phenomenon — the only non-trivial regime (saturation pinning at 1-1/N) was an artifact, honestly retracted. Part (B) reduces to a well-known cache-replacement result (recency-cap loses to content-aware eviction when the query distribution is heavy-tailed over old items — Belady 1966; LRU-vs-LFU; ARC, Megiddo & Modha 2003, https://www.usenix.org/legacy/event/fast03/tech/full_papers/megiddo/megiddo.pdf) re-derived without citing it, and the 'win' is at a hand-picked Jaccard operating point that the prereg explicitly committed to avoid. Comparator is also wrong: real agent-memory systems (MemGPT, Packer et al. 2023, https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08560; Generative Agents, Park et al. 2023, https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442) use importance-weighted eviction, not pure recency — beating the weakest baseline is not evidence for the claimed mechanism. Oracle gap mean +0.12 / max +0.80 says the lexical signal under test is not the 'semantic-dedup' the claim names. Net: the surprising result is predicted by basic theory, the assumptions are not tested, the statistical pooling masks sign-flipping, and the comparator is too weak. RED.
